# Firebase Spark — stay on free tier

Production must not depend on high-volume **Firestore** traffic. Daily limits (typical Spark): ~50K reads, ~20K writes, ~20K deletes. Quota resets at **Pacific midnight** (~4–5pm JST).

**RTDB is separate** (own Spark quotas). In this repo RTDB holds **arcade multiplayer** (`tossRooms`, `racerRooms`, `ghostRooms` / `ghostWorlds` / `ghostActions`), **teacher classroom race participants** (`classRaces/{pin}/participants`, live progress ticks), plus legacy `config`, `searchCache`, `workerActivityLogs`. The race control doc (`races/{pin}`) and `raceHistory` stay in **Firestore**.

## Do not add

- Global `setInterval` / heartbeat **writes** (presence, pings, progress ticks) on every student session
- App-wide `onSnapshot` listeners; only subscribe while the relevant UI is open, unsubscribe on leave
- Queries with `staleTime: 0` on large scans (`getAllProgress`, `getClassProgress`, full subcollections) unless behind explicit manual refresh
- Firestore writes on every keystroke, timer tick, or page visibility ping

## Do

- Gate heavy queries: `enabled` when the page/tab is active (`getActivePage()`, route, tab signal)
- Default **≥ 5 min** `staleTime` (and reasonable `gcTime`) for classroom/lessons bulk reads
- Prefer one read/write per meaningful user action (finish test, save settings), not per second
- Before shipping realtime features, estimate: `(users × writes per hour × active hours)` vs 20K/day
- If live monitoring is needed later, prefer RTDB (like arcade multiplayer) or polling—not Firestore heartbeats on Spark

## Review checklist (Firestore-touching PRs)

1. Who runs this path (all students vs teachers only)?
2. Reads/writes per user per minute at peak?
3. Still safe if 30+ students have old cached bundles for a day?
